{#
Generated by: Claude Code CLI
Date time: 2026/09/03 03:18 SAST
Version: 0.5.8.3
Description:
  The shared live search bar for every paginated dataset.

  One control, parameterised by dataset prefix, exactly as the shared pagination and universe
  controls are. There is no per-surface JavaScript anywhere: a list gets a working search by
  including this file and naming its dataset.

  How the live behaviour is achieved, and why it is done this way:

  The request attributes sit on the input itself, not on the surrounding form. They were on the
  form to begin with, listening to the input through a from selector, and the result was that
  nothing happened until the reader pressed Enter - the form's own submit was the only trigger that
  actually fired. Putting them on the element that produces the events removes that indirection
  entirely, and the include attribute still carries the rest of the form so the data scope and any
  other field travel with the search.

  The trigger is the input event rather than keyup, so pasting and the search box's own clear
  button count as typing, and clearing the box brings the whole list straight back. The delay means
  no request is made while somebody is still typing, and changed suppresses a request when the
  value has not actually changed, so arrow keys, shift and tabbing out cost nothing. The sync
  attribute aborts a request still in flight when a newer one starts, which prevents a slower
  earlier response overwriting a faster later one. All of it comes from the platform's existing
  script library rather than a second hand-written request layer, because two request mechanisms on
  one page produce races between themselves that are worse than the one being solved.

  The page number is deliberately absent from this form. A new search returns to the first page,
  and doing that by simply not sending a page is what makes the no-JavaScript path behave the same
  way; resetting it in script would leave the plain form able to search page four of a result set
  that has one page.

  Without JavaScript this is an ordinary GET form and the submit button submits it. That button
  lives inside a noscript element, so where scripting is available it is never in the document at
  all. It used to be rendered always and hidden by a script that ran once at page load, which meant
  every section arriving later by lazy load or by a swap brought a visible button nothing hid.

  Callers include this file passing the dataset prefix as ds_name, the visible label as ds_label,
  the current term as ds_value and the route as ds_action. The region ids are derived from the
  prefix by convention, so a caller cannot pair a control with the wrong table.

  The swap deliberately targets an outer wrapper that is never itself replaced, and replaces only
  the results element inside it. An earlier version replaced the targeted element directly, so one
  malformed response removed the element carrying the target id and every later search silently did
  nothing - the control was dead until the page was reloaded. A wrapper that always survives means
  a bad response costs one failed search rather than the whole surface.

  WARNING: a template comment is not a safe place to document syntax. A comment delimiter
  written inside a comment body ends the comment early and leaks the remainder onto the page.
  Keep every comment plain prose: no comment delimiters and no code, not even to document one.
